    metaTitle: "How Humidity Destroys Box Strength — and How to Engineer Against It | Bhaavya Ecopack",
    metaDescription: "Independent studies show corrugated board loses 30–40% of its compression strength at 80% relative humidity, and over 50% at 90% RH — the actual conditions in",
    category: "Moisture Science",
    datePublished: "2026-07-01",
    dateModified: "2026-07-20",
    readTime: "6 min read",
    qaQuestion: "How much strength does a corrugated box lose in humidity?",
    qaAnswer: "Independent studies show corrugated board loses 30–40% of its compression strength at 80% relative humidity, and over 50% at 90% RH — the actual conditions inside sea-freight containers and monsoon warehouses. Board moisture roughly doubles between 50% and 90% RH.",
    faqs: [{ q: "What Cobb value should export boxes have?", a: "Export-grade work typically specifies Cobb 30–40 g/m² or lower; IS 2771:2022 caps standard board at 225 g/m² over 30 minutes — a wide gap that explains many sea-freight failures." }, { q: "Do I need waterproof or moisture-resistant board?", a: "Moisture-resistant handles humid air over time; waterproof adds higher-barrier coatings for direct water contact (ice, condensation drip, washdowns). We spec by actual exposure." }, { q: "Can testing be documented per shipment?", a: "Yes — we test bursting, Cobb and GSM per lot and share records with the consignment." }],
